WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:09.480
AI can be useful for understanding code snippets, routes, handlers, controllers, middleware, and authorization checks when the assessment includes code or API documentation review.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:16.340
The assistant can summarize what a function appears to do, identify relevant data flows, or generate questions for developers.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.520
This can accelerate comprehension, especially in unfamiliar frameworks or large codebases.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:27.660
OpenAPI specifications and API documentation are good candidates for structured AI assistance.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:37.800
The assistant can group endpoints by resource, identify parameters that appear sensitive, summarize authentication requirements, and list places where authorization behavior is unclear.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:44.180
Those summaries help the assessor plan review work, but they do not prove the implementation behaves as documented.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.040
Data flow reasoning should remain evidence-driven.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:53.260
AI can help trace how input appears to move from a request to validation, business logic, storage, and output.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:01:02.680
It can also help identify assumptions, such as whether a value is trusted, whether a role check is applied consistently, or whether a dependency handles sanitization.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.380
The analyst should verify these points in code, documentation, or authorized testing.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.720
Avoid blind trust in AI-generated interpretations.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:17.340
Models can miss important framework behavior, misunderstand custom abstractions, or invent relationships that are not in the code.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:25.560
A healthy review process asks the assistant to cite the provided snippet, separate evidence from assumptions, and list follow-up questions.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.300
The goal is faster understanding, not automatic code judgment.
